      Weights only (70.6 B params × bytes/param). Actual GPU memory includes activations, KV cache, and overhead.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Quantization operates on three axes: <strong>what gets quantized</strong> (weights only, or weights + activations), <strong>how many bits</strong>, and <strong>when</strong> (during training, or after). The rest of this post maps out the full landscape.</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     §02  BIT ANATOMY
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-formats" data-code="formats">
<div class="section-no">02 — Bit Anatomy</div>
<h2>Understanding Precision Formats</h2>
<p>Every numerical format makes a fundamental tradeoff: more bits in the <em>exponent</em> gives a wider dynamic range (can represent very large and very small numbers), while more bits in the <em>mantissa</em> (significand) gives finer precision (more decimal digits of accuracy). Integer formats give up the exponent entirely — they cover a fixed range uniformly.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">bit-field explorer — click a format to inspect it</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click any button</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="fmt-btns">
      <button class="fmt-btn active" data-fmt="fp32">FP32</button>
      <button class="fmt-btn" data-fmt="bf16">BF16</button>
      <button class="fmt-btn" data-fmt="fp16">FP16</button>
      <button class="fmt-btn" data-fmt="fp8e4m3">FP8 E4M3</button>
      <button class="fmt-btn" data-fmt="fp8e5m2">FP8 E5M2</button>
      <button class="fmt-btn" data-fmt="int8">INT8</button>
      <button class="fmt-btn" data-fmt="int4">INT4</button>
