      <div class="bit-leg"><div class="bit-swatch" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,0.85)"></div>Sign (1 bit)</div>
      <div class="bit-leg"><div class="bit-swatch" style="background:rgba(139,92,246,0.8)"></div>Exponent</div>
      <div class="bit-leg"><div class="bit-swatch" style="background:rgba(6,182,212,0.75)"></div>Mantissa</div>
      <div class="bit-leg"><div class="bit-swatch" style="background:rgba(107,114,128,0.7)"></div>Integer magnitude</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>The formats in detail</h3>
<p><strong>FP32 (single precision)</strong> — the training default since the 1980s. One sign bit, 8 exponent bits (range ~±3.4×10³⁸), 23 mantissa bits (~7 significant decimal digits). Every intermediate activation, gradient, and master weight traditionally lives here.</p>
<p><strong>BF16 (bfloat16)</strong> — Google Brain's answer to mixed-precision training. Keeps FP32's 8-bit exponent (same dynamic range, so gradients don't overflow) but cuts the mantissa to 7 bits (~3 significant digits). Easy to cast between FP32 and BF16 without clipping; hardware support on A100, H100, Apple M-series.</p>
<p><strong>FP16 (half precision)</strong> — 5 exponent bits (range ±65,504), 10 mantissa bits. More decimal precision than BF16 but much smaller range — gradient overflow is common in FP16 training without loss scaling. Widely used in inference.</p>
<p><strong>FP8 E4M3</strong> — 4 exponent bits (range ±448), 3 mantissa bits. Used for <em>weights and forward activations</em> in NVIDIA Transformer Engine: the precision is prioritized over range because activations are already clipped to a bounded region after LayerNorm.</p>
<p><strong>FP8 E5M2</strong> — 5 exponent bits (range ±57,344), 2 mantissa bits. Used for <em>gradients</em> during FP8 training: gradients can be very small or very large, so wider range matters more than precision.</p>
<p><strong>INT8</strong> — a signed 8-bit integer, range −128 to +127. Uniform step size: every representable value is equally spaced. Fast on all modern hardware (dedicated INT8 tensor cores on A100+). The dominant format for post-training quantization from 2020–2023.</p>
<p><strong>INT4</strong> — 4-bit signed integer, range −8 to +7 (16 levels). Very coarse, but surprisingly effective for weight-only quantization when combined with per-group scaling. Used by GPTQ, AWQ, and the GGUF ecosystem.</p>
<p><strong>FP4 E2M1</strong> — NVIDIA Blackwell's native format: 1 sign + 2 exponent + 1 mantissa = 4 bits, range ±6.0. The float layout recovers more accuracy than INT4 at the same bit budget because the non-uniform spacing mimics real weight distributions.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <p><strong>NF4 (NormalFloat-4)</strong> — used by QLoRA. Not a hardware type; it's a quantization <em>codebook</em> designed so that each of the 16 representable values covers an equal share of a unit normal distribution. Information-theoretically optimal for Gaussian weights, but requires a lookup table.</p>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     §03  WXaY TAXONOMY
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-taxonomy" data-code="taxonomy">
<div class="section-no">03 — The WXaY Taxonomy</div>
<h2>Decoding the Notation: W4A8, FP8, W4A4</h2>
<p>The shorthand <code>WXaY</code> tells you the bit-width of <em>W</em>eights (stored on disk, loaded for each matmul) and <em>A</em>ctivations (computed at runtime). Together they determine which kind of matrix-multiply the hardware will execute.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">WXaY — the two axes of quantization</div>
  <div><span class="math-var">W</span> = weight bits &nbsp;(how weights are <em>stored</em> and loaded)</div>
  <div><span class="math-var">A</span> = activation bits &nbsp;(how inputs to each layer are <em>represented</em>)</div>
  <div style="margin-top:.5rem">
    <span class="math-var">W4A16</span> — 4-bit weights, 16-bit activations &nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← weight-only; saves memory bandwidth</span>
  </div>
  <div><span class="math-var">W8A8</span> &nbsp; — 8-bit weights &amp; activations &nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← runs INT8 GEMM; saves both memory &amp; compute</span></div>
  <div><span class="math-var">W4A8</span> &nbsp; — 4-bit weights, 8-bit activations &nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← dequantize weights at runtime, run INT8 GEMM</span></div>
  <div><span class="math-var">W4A4</span> &nbsp; — 4-bit weights &amp; activations &nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← requires specialized INT4 GEMM kernel</span></div>
</div>

<p><strong>Weight-only quantization (W4A16)</strong> is the most common deployment format today. Weights live in INT4; they are dequantized back to FP16 on-the-fly before the matrix multiply. This cuts <em>memory bandwidth</em> (the bottleneck in auto-regressive decoding) but doesn't reduce FLOPs — the GEMM itself still runs in FP16.</p>
<p><strong>Weight + activation quantization (W8A8, W4A8, W4A4)</strong> runs the GEMM in lower precision, which also reduces compute. This is the regime for hardware-accelerated inference (H100's FP8 tensor cores, Blackwell's FP4 units). However, quantizing activations is harder because they often contain <em>outliers</em> — a few channels with magnitudes 100× larger than the rest — which make naive uniform quantization lossy.</p>

<h3>Quantization mechanics: scale and zero-point</h3>
<p>Every quantization scheme maps a real-valued tensor to discrete levels and back. The standard affine (asymmetric) formula is:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">affine quantization</div>
  <div><span class="math-var">x_q</span> = round( <span class="math-var">x</span> / <span class="math-var">s</span> ) + <span class="math-var">z</span> &nbsp;&nbsp; where &nbsp; <span class="math-var">s</span> = (x_max − x_min) / (2<sup>b</sup> − 1)</div>
  <div style="margin-top:.3rem"><span class="math-var">x̂</span> = (<span class="math-var">x_q</span> − <span class="math-var">z</span>) × <span class="math-var">s</span> &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← dequantization (reconstruction)</span></div>
  <div style="margin-top:.3rem"><span class="math-var">s</span> = scale, &nbsp;<span class="math-var">z</span> = zero-point &nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← stored alongside quantized weights</span></div>
</div>

<p>For <em>symmetric</em> quantization (z = 0, used for weights), the formula simplifies to <code>x_q = round(x / s)</code>. The scale <code>s</code> is typically stored per-channel (one scale per output channel) or per-group (one scale per 128-weight block).</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">quantization error vs bit-width — click to compare</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click INT8 / INT4 / INT2</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="qbits-btns">
      <button class="qbits-btn" data-bits="8">INT8</button>
      <button class="qbits-btn active" data-bits="4">INT4</button>
      <button class="qbits-btn" data-bits="2">INT2</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="quant-canvas" height="180"></canvas>
    <p style="font-size:.7rem;color:var(--muted);margin:.5rem 0 0;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">
      Left half: original signal. Right half: quantized reconstruction. Each &ldquo;step&rdquo; is one quantization level.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Granularity: per-tensor, per-channel, per-group</h3>
<p>The granularity of the scale factor dramatically affects accuracy. A single scale for the whole tensor forces all weights to share one range — outliers push the scale large and crush small-magnitude weights to zero. Per-channel scales (one per output neuron) are standard for weight quantization. Per-group (e.g., one scale per 128 contiguous weights) is the sweet spot for aggressive INT4 quantization, used by GPTQ and AWQ.</p>

<h3>KV-cache quantization</h3>
<p>The key-value cache in transformer attention grows linearly with sequence length and batch size. At long contexts with large batches it dominates memory. Quantizing the KV cache to INT8 or even INT4 (with per-head scales) is now standard practice in frameworks like vLLM and TGI, giving 2–4× memory relief with minimal quality impact.</p>
</section>

