<h3>GPTQ — Second-Order Weight Quantization</h3>
<p>GPTQ (Frantar et al., ICLR 2023) was the first method to make 4-bit PTQ practical at scale. It processes one layer at a time, using a small calibration batch to compute an approximate Hessian (second-order curvature information). Weights are quantized one column at a time; after each quantization, the remaining weights are nudged to compensate for the error — a block-coordinate descent in the OBQ framework. Result: a 175B model in ~4 GPU hours, at 3–4 bits with minimal perplexity loss. Typical speedup vs FP16: 3.25× on A100, 4.5× on A6000.</p>

<h3>AWQ — Activation-Aware Weight Quantization</h3>
<p>AWQ (Lin et al., MLSys 2024 Best Paper) makes the key observation that not all weight channels are equally important. Only ~1% of channels are "salient" — those whose input activations are largest. Instead of storing those channels at higher precision (which would be hardware-inefficient), AWQ applies a mathematically equivalent per-channel scaling transformation before quantization, so salient weights land in a more favorable range. No backpropagation; only offline activation statistics are needed. Paired with the TinyChat inference engine, AWQ delivers 3× speedup over HuggingFace FP16 on desktop and mobile GPUs.</p>

<h3>SmoothQuant — W8A8 via Activation Migration</h3>
<p>The obstacle to quantizing <em>activations</em> (not just weights) is outliers: in most LLMs, a handful of channels have activations 100× larger than others, making INT8 quantization of the full activation tensor very lossy. SmoothQuant (Xiao et al., ICML 2023) solves this with a mathematically equivalent transformation: scale down the problematic activation channels by a factor α, and scale up the corresponding weight channels by the same factor. Weights can absorb this offline; activations become smooth enough for INT8 GEMM. Result: 1.56× speedup, 2× memory reduction, runs a 530B model on a single node.</p>

<h3>LLM.int8() / bitsandbytes</h3>
<p>Dettmers et al. (2022) introduced a different strategy for outliers: handle them in <em>mixed precision</em>. They showed that emergent outliers are concentrated in a small subset of feature dimensions (usually fewer than 0.01%). Their solution: decompose each matrix multiply into two parts — the outlier dimensions computed in FP16, the rest in INT8 — and sum. This keeps INT8's memory savings while preserving accuracy. Integrated into HuggingFace as <code>load_in_8bit=True</code>.</p>

<h3>QuaRot — Rotation-Based W4A4</h3>
<p>QuaRot (Ashkboos et al., 2024) pushes all the way to true 4-bit weights AND 4-bit activations, including the KV cache. The trick: apply random rotation matrices to the hidden states (mathematically equivalent to multiplying by an orthogonal matrix). Rotations redistribute outliers so uniformly that standard INT4 quantization works without any channel being retained at higher precision. On LLaMA-2-70B in W4A4, WikiText-2 perplexity loss is ≤0.47 — remarkably small for fully 4-bit inference.</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     §05  QAT
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-qat" data-code="qat">
<div class="section-no">05 — Quantization-Aware Training</div>
<h2>QAT: Train the Model to Live at Low Precision</h2>
<p>PTQ is fast but introduces error post-hoc. Quantization-aware training (QAT) instead <em>simulates</em> quantization during the forward pass while training, so the model learns to compensate for the precision loss. The result is usually better accuracy at the same bit-width — at the cost of a full or partial training run.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">PTQ vs QAT — the key workflow difference</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="pipe-cols">
      <div>
        <div class="pipe-col-head" style="color:var(--cyan)">Post-Training Quantization (PTQ)</div>
        <div class="pipe-step pipe-c"><div class="pipe-num">1</div><div class="pipe-text"><b>Pre-trained model</b> (FP32/BF16)</div></div>
        <div class="pipe-step pipe-c"><div class="pipe-num">2</div><div class="pipe-text"><b>Calibration</b>: run ~128–512 samples through the model to gather activation statistics (per-channel amax, outlier channels)</div></div>
        <div class="pipe-step pipe-c"><div class="pipe-num">3</div><div class="pipe-text"><b>Compute scales</b>: per-channel or per-group scale &amp; zero-point from calibration stats</div></div>
        <div class="pipe-step pipe-c"><div class="pipe-num">4</div><div class="pipe-text"><b>Quantize weights</b>: optionally with Hessian compensation (GPTQ), activation smoothing (SmoothQuant), or channel scaling (AWQ)</div></div>
        <div class="pipe-step pipe-c"><div class="pipe-num">5</div><div class="pipe-text"><b>Deploy</b>: quantized model, no further training needed. Hours total for 70B.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="pipe-col-head" style="color:var(--purple)">Quantization-Aware Training (QAT)</div>
        <div class="pipe-step pipe-p"><div class="pipe-num">1</div><div class="pipe-text"><b>Pre-trained model</b> (optional: start from scratch for extreme precision)</div></div>
        <div class="pipe-step pipe-p"><div class="pipe-num">2</div><div class="pipe-text"><b>Insert fake-quantize ops</b>: every weight and activation goes through <code>round(x/s)×s</code> in the forward pass</div></div>
        <div class="pipe-step pipe-p"><div class="pipe-num">3</div><div class="pipe-text"><b>Train with STE</b>: the round() op has zero gradient; the Straight-Through Estimator (STE) passes gradients through as if round() = identity</div></div>
        <div class="pipe-step pipe-p"><div class="pipe-num">4</div><div class="pipe-text"><b>Scale learning</b>: scales themselves are learned or updated via EMA of activation amaxes, co-adapting with the weights</div></div>
        <div class="pipe-step pipe-p"><div class="pipe-num">5</div><div class="pipe-text"><b>Export</b>: remove fake-quant ops, quantize weights to target format. Training cost: typically 1–5% of full pre-training.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>LLM-QAT (Meta, 2023)</h3>
<p>Meta's LLM-QAT applies QAT to LLaMA models using a data-free distillation approach: the original FP16 model generates synthetic training data (its own outputs), which is then used to fine-tune the quantized model. This sidesteps privacy concerns around training data access. They quantize weights, activations, and KV cache simultaneously, demonstrating competitive quality at W4A8KV4 and even W4A4KV4 on LLaMA-7B and 13B.</p>

<h3>BitNet b1.58 — Ternary Weights from Scratch</h3>
<p>BitNet b1.58 (Ma et al., 2024) takes QAT to the extreme: every weight in the network is constrained to &#123;&minus;1, 0, +1&#125; during training (1.58 bits per weight). The key insight is that the model must be trained from scratch in this constrained space — converting an FP32 model to ternary weights via PTQ doesn't work. The result is a model that can be served using 1-bit matrix multiplications (additions and sign flips only), enabling extreme energy efficiency on specialized hardware.</p>

<h3>MXFP8 and MXFP4 — Microsoft's Microscaling Format</h3>
<p>The OCP MX (Microscaling) specification, co-authored by Microsoft, NVIDIA, AMD and others, adds a shared exponent per block of 32 elements on top of individual FP8 or FP4 values. This block-level scaling dramatically increases the effective dynamic range without the overhead of per-element scaling. MXFP8 is supported in NVIDIA Hopper via Transformer Engine's block scaling recipe; MXFP4 is the format behind Blackwell's nvfp4. Training directly in MXFP8 has been shown to match BF16 quality across diverse model sizes.</p>
</section>

