<h3>NVIDIA Hopper (H100) — FP8 with Transformer Engine</h3>
<p>The H100 introduced native FP8 tensor cores (compute capability 9.0), capable of executing GEMMs in FP8 at 2× the throughput of FP16 and 4× BF16 FLOPS. To make this practical, NVIDIA shipped the <strong>Transformer Engine</strong> library: it wraps each transformer layer, choosing FP8 or BF16 automatically per-operation, maintaining per-tensor scaling factors (amaxes), and fusing the quantization/dequantization into the GEMM kernel itself.</p>
<p>In Transformer Engine, the forward pass uses <strong>E4M3</strong> (higher precision, smaller range) for weights and activations. The backward pass uses <strong>E5M2</strong> (wider range) for gradients. Scaling factors are computed via <em>delayed scaling</em> — using the amax from the previous iteration — or <em>current scaling</em> with a synchronization barrier.</p>
<p>DeepSeek V3, trained in early 2025, is notable for being the first widely-deployed frontier model whose <em>training</em> used FP8 (via a customized mixed-precision recipe), not just inference. At 671B parameters, the FP8 training reduced communication and compute overhead significantly versus BF16.</p>

<h3>NVIDIA Blackwell (B200/B100) — FP4 Native Compute</h3>
<p>Blackwell introduced the first GPU with native FP4 (nvfp4) tensor cores. The <strong>nvfp4</strong> format uses the E2M1 structure (1 sign + 2 exponent + 1 mantissa) together with MXFP4 microscaling: every 32-element block shares a single FP8 scaling exponent, providing enough dynamic range for practical use. NVIDIA claims ~2× the FP8 throughput on FP4 GEMMs — meaning a Blackwell GPU in FP4 mode can run at 4× the H100's FP8 throughput.</p>
<p>Both TensorRT-LLM and NVIDIA's ModelOpt library support automatic W4A8FP4 (4-bit weights with FP4 quantization, 8-bit activations) for Llama, Mistral, Mixtral, and other popular architectures, with model quality within ~1% of FP16 baselines on standard benchmarks.</p>

<h3>A100 and Older — INT8 Only</h3>
<p>The A100 has INT8 tensor cores (introduced with Turing/A30 for INT8 inference) but no native FP8 compute. On A100, FP8 operations are emulated via FP16 with software scaling — you get memory savings but not compute speedup. For A100 deployments, W8A8 INT8 (SmoothQuant) or W4A16 INT4 (GPTQ/AWQ) are the practical choices.</p>

<h3>The Scale Storage Overhead</h3>
<p>One often-overlooked cost of quantization: the scale factors. A W4A8 quantized model with per-group-128 scales stores one BF16 scale per 128 INT4 weights. For a 70B model: 70.6B weights / 128 × 2 bytes ≈ 1.1 GB of scales. Small compared to the 35.3 GB of INT4 weights, but non-negligible. Per-tensor scaling (one scale per layer) has negligible overhead but worse accuracy; per-channel scaling is the practical middle ground.</p>
</section>

