<h3>Real-World Examples</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Meta Llama-3.1 405B (FP8)</strong> — Meta's official inference uses FP8, fitting the 405B model on 8× H100 vs 16× for BF16. Released as a pre-quantized HuggingFace checkpoint via NeuralMagic.</li>
  <li><strong>DeepSeek V3 (FP8)</strong> — 671B MoE model trained and served in FP8 on H800 clusters. The custom FP8 training recipe kept accuracy within 0.1% of BF16 baseline.</li>
  <li><strong>Mistral 7B (INT4 GGUF)</strong> — runs on a single 6 GB GPU via llama.cpp GGUF Q4_K_M quantization. The de facto format for local LLM inference on consumer hardware.</li>
  <li><strong>Mixtral 8×7B (W4A16)</strong> — vLLM serves this at W4A16 AWQ with 2× throughput vs BF16, fitting the model on 2× A100 40 GB instead of 4×.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Practical Tips</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Use FP8 if you have H100/H200.</strong> The accuracy loss is negligible (&lt;0.5%), the speedup is real, and every major framework (vLLM, TGI, TensorRT-LLM) supports it natively.</li>
  <li><strong>First/last layers need higher precision.</strong> The embedding layer and the final LM head are highly sensitive to quantization. Most frameworks keep them in BF16 by default, even when all other layers are in FP8 or INT4.</li>
  <li><strong>KV cache quantization is free money.</strong> Quantizing the KV cache to INT8 (or even INT4) often gives a 2× memory reduction at long sequences with minimal quality impact. Always try it before scaling out hardware.</li>
  <li><strong>Per-group scaling beats per-tensor for INT4.</strong> Group size 128 is the standard — it adds ~3% overhead in scale storage but recovers most of the accuracy lost vs per-channel.</li>
  <li><strong>Calibration data matters for W8A8.</strong> Use a diverse calibration set (200–512 examples from the actual deployment distribution). Using only short sequences when the deployment involves long contexts will produce bad activation statistics.</li>
</ul>
</section>

